    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. When we're looking at the semi-metallic pads, we've been seeing some really erratic temperature spikes during the endurance phase—it's almost like the heat isn't dissipating predictably, and the system just seems to act up or behave inconsistently under high load. Is there a specific threshold where the heat soak causes that kind of unpredictable behavior in the friction material?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Erratic temperature spikes in semi-metallic pads often occur when the friction material nears its thermal saturation point, where the ability of the material to absorb and conduct heat diminishes rapidly. This unpredictable behavior can be related to changes in the chemical state of the pad material itself, particularly if the binding agents begin to degrade under extreme, sustained heat soak. Essentially, once the heat capacity is exceeded, the material begins to lose its consistent frictional properties as its internal temperature rises unevenly.",
      "source": "target",
